    "chunk_text": "People caring for those sick with Ebola, whether clinicians or family members, are at serious risk of infection. Transmission is also associated with funeral rites that involve washing and preparing for burial the bodies of those who died of Ebola.\n\nTo understand why Ebola is so lethal, one must look at what happens inside the body. According to the literature available on the internet, after entering through broken skin, mucous membranes, or bodily fluids, the virus targets the immune system.\u00a0 Instead of triggering a normal, controlled immune response, Ebola manipulates cells to become virus factories, spreading infection throughout the body.\n\nThis sets off a catastrophic chain reaction. The body releases massive amounts of inflammatory molecules in an uncontrolled attempt to fight the virus. This \u201cstorm\u201d damages blood vessels, causing them to leak.\u00a0 Platelets, which help blood clot, become dysfunctional, leading to both clotting in some parts of the body and uncontrolled bleeding in others.\u00a0 Blood clots are semisolid or gel-like masses that form in one\u2019s arteries and veins. They help control bleeding. But they may also cause serious issues, including deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism and heart attack. A pulmonary embolism is a blood clot that blocks and stops blood flow to an artery in the lung.\n\nThe liver, kidneys, and other organs suffer severe damage due to Ebola, impairing their function. As fluids leak out of blood vessels and blood pressure plummets, the body goes into shock. Without rapid intervention, death often follows.\n\nEbola belongs to a group of viral \u201chaemorrhagic\u201d fever diseases.\u00a0 Haemorrhagic fever combines bleeding (haemorrhaging) and fever. Fatality rates vary among the four Ebola viruses. In the absence of any treatment, the fatality rates are up to 90% for the Zaire Ebola virus, 50% for the Sudan virus, and 30% for the Bundibugyo virus."
  },
