    "num": "dailymirrorlk-e820df58",
    "date_str": "2026-06-05",
    "description": "Beyond IMF dependence",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Beyond-IMF-dependence/172-342018",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1780611845.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-06-05-dailymirrorlk-e820df58-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "195108b57c620979824e5ebe900dab56",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1049,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka cannot tax its way to prosperity. It must grow its way to prosperity while maintaining fiscal discipline. Revenue reforms and growth-oriented policies should therefore proceed hand in hand. The broader question concerns the country\u2019s future relationship with the IMF. No nation wishes to remain under an IMF programme indefinitely. Such programmes are designed as temporary mechanisms to restore stability, not permanent economic management frameworks.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s ultimate goal should be economic sovereignty built on sound fundamentals. That means reaching a point where the country no longer requires IMF assistance because it has developed the discipline and institutional strength to manage its finances independently.\n\nThe IMF has provided the breathing space needed to avert disaster. The challenge now is to use that opportunity wisely. The country should certainly aspire to come out of the IMF programme. But it should do so only after ensuring that the habits which led to economic collapse are firmly consigned to history."
  },
