    "chunk_id": "2026-06-06-islandlk-3e02ec93-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5b8fc392d23ce562dd1f2c349fc93e89",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1775,
    "chunk_text": "Forty Years After the Kantale Tank Disaster, Sri Lanka Still Awaits a Dam Safety Regulator\n\nApril 20 this year marked the 40th anniversary of one of the darkest and least discussed engineering disasters in Sri Lanka\u2019s history \u2013 the failure of the Kantale Tank bund in 1986.\n\nMore than 129 people lost their lives when a section of the dam breached in the early hours of the morning, unleashing a wall of water on unsuspecting communities downstream. To this day, the exact number of deaths remains uncertain. Many victims were never formally accounted for, and countless families who lost homes, crops, livestock and livelihoods were left to rebuild their lives with little recognition and inadequate compensation. The rehabilitation cost was estimated to cost in excess of Rs 600 million in 1988 terms.\n\nThe tragedy occurred around 3.30am, when most residents were asleep. The darkness and speed of the flood left virtually no time for warning or evacuation. Survivors have recounted harrowing stories of clinging to trees to escape the torrent while elderly relatives and young children were swept away. Entire villages were devastated. Families who had spent generations cultivating the fertile lands nourished by the tank found themselves destitute overnight.\n\nForty years later, the disaster has largely faded from public memory. Yet the questions surrounding the failure remain as relevant today as they were in 1986.\n\nA Presidential Commission of Inquiry was conducted following the disaster. However, the findings have never been publicly discussed in any meaningful way and the report itself appears to have disappeared from public view. Over the years, speculation has persisted that the findings would expose serious failures in governance and decision-making."
  },
