    "chunk_text": "A Presidential Commission of Inquiry was conducted following the disaster. However, the findings have never been publicly discussed in any meaningful way and the report itself appears to have disappeared from public view. Over the years, speculation has persisted that the findings would expose serious failures in governance and decision-making.\n\nSuch works would be considered highly questionable under accepted dam engineering principles because any penetration through a dam embankment can create pathways for seepage and internal erosion. This decision represented not merely poor judgement but a profound breach of basic dam safety practice. Unsurprisingly, a sinkhole had appeared in the dam embankment after the pump house was constructed.\n\nReports at the time also indicated that unusual seepage of water had been observed from the embankment months before the breach. Local residents reportedly raised concerns, and there were even newspaper reports drawing attention to the problem. In dam engineering, unexplained seepage is never a matter to be ignored. It is often one of the earliest indicators of internal erosion and potential instability.\n\nThe inquiry found that pile driving was very likely a significant contributor to the dam\u2019s failure.\n\nWhether every detail of the structure\u2019s failure can still be proven today is perhaps less important than the broader lesson. The Kantale disaster was not simply a natural event. It was a preventable tragedy that appears to have involved irresponsible construction on a dam wall, ignored warning signs, inadequate oversight and failures in accountability.\n\nWhat makes this even more concerning is that Sri Lanka still lacks a comprehensive dam safety regulatory framework."
  },
