    "md5": "2b54c0e7dbfac51ab04e73bd5f27f284",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1745,
    "chunk_text": "Whether every detail of the structure\u2019s failure can still be proven today is perhaps less important than the broader lesson. The Kantale disaster was not simply a natural event. It was a preventable tragedy that appears to have involved irresponsible construction on a dam wall, ignored warning signs, inadequate oversight and failures in accountability.\n\nWhat makes this even more concerning is that Sri Lanka still lacks a comprehensive dam safety regulatory framework.\n\nFor more than 2,000 years, Sri Lankan kings and engineers constructed reservoirs and canals that transformed dry landscapes into productive agricultural regions. Thousands of tanks and dams remain in operation today, a testament to the ingenuity and skill of our ancestors.\n\nYet while we celebrate this remarkable hydraulic heritage, we have failed to establish a modern regulatory system to ensure these structures remain safe.\n\nApart from the ancient dams, Sri Lanka\u2019s other dams constructed in more recent periods are now many decades old. They require continuous monitoring, periodic safety reviews, risk assessments, emergency action plans and independent oversight. At present, dam owners are often responsible for regulating themselves. This creates an obvious gap in accountability.\n\nThe need for stronger oversight is not confined to the risk of dam failure alone. In November 2025, Cyclone Ditwah highlighted the importance of emergency preparedness and public warning systems associated with major dams. Communities living downstream of Kotmale Reservoir reportedly received little or no warning before large floods from rainfall and water releases from the reservoir inundated downstream areas. This resulted in loss of many lives and destruction of homes."
  },
