    "md5": "45d6b4306b16403081afa7e9ced5fc4f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1679,
    "chunk_text": "The need for stronger oversight is not confined to the risk of dam failure alone. In November 2025, Cyclone Ditwah highlighted the importance of emergency preparedness and public warning systems associated with major dams. Communities living downstream of Kotmale Reservoir reportedly received little or no warning before large floods from rainfall and water releases from the reservoir inundated downstream areas. This resulted in loss of many lives and destruction of homes.\n\nOther countries have recognised this challenge and acted decisively. India, which manages more than 5,000 large dams, enacted the Dam Safety Act in 2021, establishing national and state-level institutions responsible for surveillance, inspection, maintenance and safety oversight. The legislation created formal mechanisms to prevent dam failure disasters and improve public safety.\n\nThere have been reports that a Cabinet paper proposing the establishment of a national dam safety regulator was approved several years ago. Yet little appears to have happened since. If true, this represents a missed opportunity to address a critical national risk.\n\nThe argument that a regulator would be too costly is unconvincing. Sri Lanka already possesses significant expertise within the Irrigation Department, Mahaweli Authority, Ceylon Electricity Board, National Building Research Organisation, universities and the private engineering sector. A regulator could be established through a new governance framework that draws upon existing expertise while providing independent oversight and accountability. The cost would be negligible compared with the economic and human consequences of a major dam failure."
  },
