    "md5": "a0c7db94b3f958002d085cb563fb9074",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1957,
    "chunk_text": "The argument that a regulator would be too costly is unconvincing. Sri Lanka already possesses significant expertise within the Irrigation Department, Mahaweli Authority, Ceylon Electricity Board, National Building Research Organisation, universities and the private engineering sector. A regulator could be established through a new governance framework that draws upon existing expertise while providing independent oversight and accountability. The cost would be negligible compared with the economic and human consequences of a major dam failure.\n\nThe responsibility of government extends beyond building infrastructure. It includes ensuring that infrastructure remains safe throughout its life. Citizens have a right to expect that known risks will be identified, monitored and managed before disaster strikes.\n\nMore than 3,700 years ago, King Hammurabi of Babylon recognised this principle when he enacted laws governing the construction and maintenance of irrigation works. His code stated that if a man neglected to maintain a dam and its failure caused flooding and destruction, he would be held personally responsible for the damage. This principle remains relevant today: those entrusted with public infrastructure have a duty of care to protect the lives and livelihoods of the people who depend upon it.\n\nForty years after the Kantale disaster, Sri Lanka owes a debt to the victims and their families. The greatest tribute we can pay them is not a memorial plaque or an anniversary speech. It is ensuring that such a tragedy never happens again.\n\nThe establishment of an independent national dam safety regulator would be a significant step in that direction. It would demonstrate that the lessons of Kantale have finally been learned and that public safety is being treated with the seriousness it deserves.\n\nThe author is a water policy and dam safety specialist who established and led Victoria\u2019s (Australia) dam safety regulatory program."
