    "chunk_size_bytes": 1320,
    "chunk_text": "In her evidence, it was revealed that the mother never intended to transfer the beneficial ownership of the property and expected her son to recover it once the bank loan had been settled. She subsequently instituted legal action in 1997, seeking a declaration that her son held the property in trust for her and an order compelling its transfer back to her.\n\nThe son, however, contended that he had genuinely purchased the property for Rs. 125,000 and denied that the transfer was merely a financing arrangement.\n\nThe Court observed that while the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and the Evidence Ordinance generally prohibit oral evidence from contradicting the terms of a written deed, Section 83 permits courts to consider surrounding circumstances to determine whether a transferor truly intended to part with the beneficial interest in a property.\n\nAfter reviewing the evidence, the Court found that several factors supported the mother's claim. These included evidence that she had originally purchased the land and built the house with considerable difficulty, that the property was her only residence, and that the bank loan obtained through the transfer was used to expand the family home.\n\nThe Supreme Court held that the mother had not intended to transfer the beneficial ownership of the property to her son."
