    "chunk_text": "Tighter dollar conversion rule for exporters weighs on Colombo stock trading\n\nThe Central Bank\u2019s directive requiring exporters to convert their dollars within one month \u2013 down from three months \u2013 amid rising depreciation pressure on the currency, sparked concerns among stock market participants yesterday, analysts said.\n\nAmid those\u00a0 developments the ASPI was down 85.54 points at 21,461.63, while the more liquid S&P SL20 was down 0.15 percent, or 9.12 points, at 5,991.56.\n\nMarket turnover was Ra 1.67 billion with five crossings.\u00a0 Those crossings were reported in JKH, crossed 13.6 million shares to the tune of Rs 259 million and its share price traded at Rs 19.70, Janashakthi 4.5 million shares crossed to the tune of Rs\u00a0 54 million and its share price traded at Rs 12, CCS 383,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 52\u00a0 million and its share price traded at Rs 135.50, Cheveron Lubricant 185,500 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 37 million and its share price traded at Rs 190.75 and LMF 300,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 25.5 million and its share price traded at Rs 85.\n\nIn the retail market top seven companies that have mainly contributed to the turnover were Cheveron Lubricant Rs 166 million (852,000 shares traded), Commercial Bank Rs 113 million (554,000 shares traded), Pan Asia Power Rs 66 million (four million shares traded), JKH Rs 56 million (2.8 million shares traded), Prime Lands Residencies Rs 53 million (1.6 million shares traded), Cables Solutions Rs Rs 45 million (four million shares traded)\u00a0 and Dialog Axiata Rs 43 million (970,000 shares traded). During the day 9.2 million shares volumes changed hands in 16705 transactions.Capital goods led turnover with\u00a0 Rs 469.3 million .\n\nPositive contributors to the ASPI were Bukit Darah (up 6.02 percent at 845.50 rupees), JKH (up 0.51 percent at Rs 19.80 ), Aitken Spence (up 1.48 percent at Rs 137.00 ), and Associated Motor Finance Company (up 3.33 percent at Rs 46.50 )."
