    "chunk_text": "Some 200 mutual aid officers will be on the ground from Thursday and on Wednesday afternoon Chief Constable Jon Boutcher insisted those responsible for the disorder will be dealt with according to the law.\n\nLittle-Pengelly told BBC Breakfast that while people in Northern Ireland and across the UK have concerns around immigration and vetting, people are \"trying to use those genuinely held concerns in the wider community to try to bring that disorder and violence, and indeed at times racism to the streets of Belfast\".\n\n\"My message is very clear to those people who are intent on doing so \u2013 they do absolutely nothing to support the cause of getting those issues amplified.\"\n\nThreats have been made to healthcare staff and a nurse was chased into a hospital by masked men, a union has said.\n\nPatricia McKeown from Unison said on Wednesday evening a nurse \"with a different skin colour\" was chased into the Ulster Hospital by four masked men.\n\nShe said it was \"it's not good enough to call for calm\" and leaders must take action.\n\nThe union has also said that health workers have been left terrified after a \"threat\" was made against those from overseas who work at Whiteabbey hospital.\n\n\"When we say the call to arms on social media we knew our overseas members would be under threat,\" McKeown told BBC Radio Ulster's Good Morning Ulster programme.\n\nWednesday's violence was not to the same extent as Tuesday nights that saw condemnation from politicians and police.\n\nA Department for Infrastructure depot was broken into and a vehicle set alight at Sandyknowes Belfast.\n\nThe Minister Liz Kimmins condemned it and the disorder was \"disgraceful and disgusting\".\n\nPrime Minister Sir Keir Starmer said those behind the violence \"will feel the full force of the law\"\n\nIn Londonderry, police advised motorists to avoid the Ardmore Road at the Church Brae junction due to items on fire.\n\nThere were also peaceful protests at Stormont Estate and Coleraine."
